course: dictionary
lesson: 7768
title: Pus and infection
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Pus is a thick yellow or greenish liquid that forms in a cut or sore when it becomes infected. «A boy in Leeds did not clean a graze from the playground; two days later it was red, hot and full of pus.» The clues red, hot and full show that germs got in and caused trouble.
explain: Pus is made of dead white blood cells, germs and bits of tissue. White blood cells are the body's fighters. They go to the wound to battle the germs, and the dead cells and germs gather together as pus.
explain: Pus is a warning sign, so do not ignore it. If a cut becomes red, hot, swollen or full of pus, tell a parent, teacher or doctor. Do not pick at the scab: «The scab is the body's own plaster, so leave it alone.»
fact: Pus is a thick yellow or greenish liquid.
fact: Pus forms in a cut or sore that is infected.
fact: Pus is made of dead white blood cells, germs and bits of tissue.
fact: «Infected» means germs have got into a wound and are causing trouble.
fact: If a cut is «red, hot, swollen or full of pus», tell an adult.
exercise: «A boy in Bristol bit his nails until a fingertip became red, swollen and full of pus.» What does full of pus tell us? | The nail has grown longer | The finger is clean and healing well | *The finger is infected and needs a doctor | The finger is cold
exercise: Pus is made of what? | Mud from the playground | Only air | *Dead white blood cells, germs and bits of tissue | Sugar and water
exercise: A cut is red, hot and swollen. What should you do? | Cover it with dirt | *Tell a parent or a doctor | Ignore it, it will go away on its own | Pick off the scab to let it out
test: In these lessons, what does «infected» mean? | Clean and dry | Covered in mud | A kind of sticking plaster | *Germs have got into a wound and are causing trouble
test: What are the white blood cells doing when pus forms? | Making the skin turn green on purpose | *Fighting germs, and the dead cells gather as pus | Making the cut bigger | Cooling the skin down
exam: Which is the best thing to do if a cut becomes red, hot, swollen and full of pus? | Squeeze the pus out at home | Leave it alone and hope it stops | *Tell an adult, because it may need a doctor | Wrap it tightly in a sock
